THE REORDER (task item 1). Before: joycon_init() ran, inside one `if (joycon_using_usb(ctlr) && !joycon_send_usb(ctlr, JC_USB_CMD_HANDSHAKE, HZ))` branch: log 'detected USB controller', then unconditionally send JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M, then JC_USB_CMD_HANDSHAKE again, then JC_USB_CMD_NO_TIMEOUT; the `else if (jc_type_is_chrggrip(ctlr))` branch failed immediately; only after that whole if/else did the code call `joycon_read_info(ctlr)` and abort on failure. After: the same handshake `if`/`else if (chrggrip)` runs first (now just logging on success, with no baudrate work inside it), then `joycon_read_info(ctlr)` runs unconditionally (same failure handling: `hid_err(...); goto out_unlock;`), and only after read_info succeeds does a *new*, separate `if (joycon_using_usb(ctlr) && !joycon_device_is_8bitdo(ctlr))` block run the baudrate-set / handshake-confirm / no-timeout sequence that used to be nested in the first block. The discriminator is a new inline helper: `static inline bool joycon_device_is_8bitdo(struct joycon_ctlr *ctlr) { return !strncmp(ctlr->mac_addr_str, "E4:17:D8", 8); }` -- `mac_addr_str` is populated by `joycon_read_info()` from the JC_SUBCMD_REQ_DEV_INFO reply (`hid_info(ctlr->hdev, "controller MAC = %s
", ctlr->mac_addr_str);` immediately precedes joycon_init() in the source, confirmed by reading the function above joycon_init in the patched tree). E4:17:D8 is 8BitDo's OUI; the commit's own A/B on real hardware read back 'E4:17:D8:F9:77:78' as the adapter's own MAC (not the paired controller's), confirming the adapter answers the device-info subcommand from its own firmware regardless of controller wake state.

BEHAVIOUR ON joycon_read_info() TIMEOUT (task item 3). Quoted from the patched, fully-stacked 6.18 tree built above:
```
	/* needed to retrieve the controller type */
	ret = joycon_read_info(ctlr);
	if (ret) {
		hid_err(hdev, "Failed to retrieve controller info; ret=%d\n",
			ret);
		goto out_unlock;
	}

	if (joycon_using_usb(ctlr) && !joycon_device_is_8bitdo(ctlr)) {
		/* set baudrate for improved latency */
		ret = joycon_send_usb(ctlr, JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M, HZ);
```
On a read_info timeout (ret=-110/ETIMEDOUT, the 'Failed to get joycon info; ret=-110' the commit message discusses) the probe aborts via `goto out_unlock` -- the *same* error path, same message shape, same eventual probe failure as pre-patch (the pre-patch tree has the identical `if (ret) { hid_err(...); goto out_unlock; }` block, just positioned after the baudrate work instead of before it). So the reorder does NOT change whether the probe fails on a read_info timeout, and does not change the error message. What it DOES change: pre-patch, the baudrate/handshake-confirm/no-timeout sequence ran (and could already have triggered the 8BitDo reset-loop) *before* read_info was ever reached, so a subsequent read_info timeout was measured on a device that had already been sent the offending command; post-patch, if read_info times out, the baudrate block is never reached at all (it is now gated behind the successful read_info return) -- the device is left completely untouched by JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M/HANDSHAKE/NO_TIMEOUT when this happens. This is exactly what the commit message's own testing note describes: 'an arm only exercises this patch if joycon_read_info() succeeded... An arm that logs "Failed to get joycon info; ret=-110" never reached the baudrate command and measured nothing, so the patched and unpatched kernels look identical' -- 'look identical' in the sense that both arms fail probe the same way for that specific run, not that the intervening command sequence was identical.

GENUINE PRO CONTROLLER PROBE PATH (task item 3, second half). For a real Pro Controller (`mac_addr_str` never matches the E4:17:D8 prefix, so `joycon_device_is_8bitdo()` is always false), every operation the old code performed is still performed -- USB handshake, baudrate set, handshake-confirm, no-timeout ping, read_info -- with NO operation skipped or added. The only change is *order*: read_info now runs between the initial handshake and the baudrate/confirm/no-timeout sequence, instead of after all of it. The one behavioural nuance: if a genuine Pro Controller's read_info happens to time out (the commit's own 'What makes read_info time out has not been isolated' paragraph -- observed as an intermittent, uncharacterized failure unrelated to the 8BitDo bug, not reproduced by power-cycling the pad across two reboots), the patched kernel now aborts probe *before* ever sending JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M/HANDSHAKE/NO_TIMEOUT, whereas the unpatched kernel would already have sent them and left the controller in 3M-baud/no-timeout mode before the later read_info failure aborted probe. Probe fails in both cases either way; only the controller's transient wire-protocol state at the moment of failure differs. Not exercised on our hardware (task item 7 gap).

MAINLINE STATUS (task item 4). `grep -n "E4:17:D8\|8bitdo\|8BitDo" $S/mainline/drivers/hid/hid-nintendo.c` (torvalds/linux, v7.3-rc2 area, HEAD 50d05c7c76c9) returns nothing -- absent. Confirmed structurally too: mainline's joycon_init() still has JC_USB_CMD_BAUDRATE_3M at line 2528, *before* joycon_read_info(ctlr) at line 2554 -- the pre-patch order, unreordered. `git -C $S/linux log --oneline v6.18.39..HEAD -- drivers/hid/hid-nintendo.c` (HEAD = v6.18.49) lists exactly three stable commits, none of which touch the probe/baudrate path: `5efcd7bbf HID: nintendo: stop device IO before hid_hw_stop on probe failure` (moves error-path cleanup after joycon_init()/joycon_leds_create() failures -- adds `hid_device_io_stop()` before `hid_hw_close()`, does not touch joycon_init() itself), `268679f50 HID: nintendo: register input device after capabilities are set` (moves `input_register_device()` inside joycon_input_create(), unrelated function), `51cfd1adb HID: nintendo: fix out-of-bounds read in joycon_ctlr_read_handler()` (bounds-check fix in the IMU/subcmd report-parsing path, unrelated function). None of the three collide with or supersede this reorder; no re-anchoring risk from the .39-.49 stable drift for this specific carry.

HARDWARE-GATED (task item 7). Re-verifying this carry on our own kernel needs an 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter in Switch mode (presenting 057e:2009) plus a paired 8BitDo controller -- neither is on hand in this environment. Per env.md's grounding gap, the 6.18 tree used above is v6.18.49 (release commit 1c732c6b94f0, linux-6.18.y), not the v6.18.50 the pin actually targets; v6.18.50 is unverified from this session (kernel.org blocked, gregkh mirror one release behind). The apply-order proof above is therefore sound for 6.18.49 and stated as such; a v6.18.50 re-check is the same open item PLAN.md Sec 3 tracks for the whole stack, not something specific to this patch (none of the three v6.18.39-49 stable commits touched the probe path this patch reorders, so a .49-to-.50 bump is unlikely to change the apply-order result, but that is an inference, not a measurement -- the record does not claim .50 was tested).

